<zod-v4-guidelines>

<whats-new-in-v4>
<major-improvements>
- **14x Faster String Parsing**: Significant performance boost
- **7x Faster Array Parsing**: Optimized array validation
- **6.5x Faster Object Parsing**: Enhanced object validation speed
- **57% Smaller Bundle**: Reduced core bundle size
- **20x Fewer TS Instantiations**: Faster type-checking and IDE performance
- **@zod/mini Package**: Lightweight 1.9KB gzipped version for frontends
- **Global Registry**: Named schema management for large apps
- **Custom Metadata**: Support for form generation and schema-driven systems
- **Built-in JSON Schema**: Native JSON Schema support
- **Enhanced Error Messages**: Pretty-printing for readable validation errors
</major-improvements>
</whats-new-in-v4>

<critical-import-rule>
<requirement>Always import from 'zod/v4', never 'zod'</requirement>
<installation>
```bash
npm install zod@next

# For lightweight frontend use
npm install @zod/mini
```
</installation>
</critical-import-rule>

<core-rules>

<rule>
<name>Type Inference</name>
<requirement>Every schema MUST have inferred type above it</requirement>
<example type="good">
```typescript
export type User = z.infer<typeof User>
export const User = z.object({...})
```
</example>
<requirements>
- ALWAYS place type above schema
- ALWAYS same name for type & schema
- NEVER use "Schema" suffix
- ALWAYS use JSDoc (/** */), never //
- NO EXCEPTIONS - even for internal/helper schemas
</requirements>
</rule>

<rule>
<name>String Validation</name>
<requirement>String validations are standalone functions</requirement>
<example type="comparison">
```typescript
// WRONG: z.string().email()
// RIGHT: z.email(), z.url(), z.uuid(), z.ip()
```
</example>
</rule>

<rule>
<name>Error Messages</name>
<requirement>Use `error` param sparingly - Zod's defaults are excellent</requirement>
<example type="comparison">
```typescript
// WRONG: z.email({error: "Invalid email"}) // Redundant!
// RIGHT: z.email() // Zod says "Invalid email"
// RIGHT: Only for business logic:
z.string().check((val) => /[A-Z]/.test(val), {
	error: 'Must contain uppercase',
})
```
</example>
</rule>

<rule>
<name>Number Types</name>
<requirements>
- Use z.number() for general numbers
- z.int() for integers only (not z.number().int())
- z.int32(), z.float64() for specific types
- Numbers finite by default
</requirements>
</rule>

<rule>
<name>Object Types</name>
<types>
- `z.object()` - strips unknowns (default)
- `z.strictObject()` - rejects extras
- `z.looseObject()` - allows extras
</types>
</rule>

<rule>
<name>Custom Validation</name>
<requirement>Use .check() not .superRefine()</requirement>
<migration-steps>
- `val` → `ctx.value`
- `ctx.addIssue()` → `ctx.issues.push()`
- `z.ZodIssueCode.custom` → `'custom'`
- Add `input: ctx.value` to issue object
- Use .check() for detailed errors, .refine() for simple validation
</migration-steps>
</rule>

<rule>
<name>Error Formatting</name>
<methods>
- `z.prettifyError()` - Human-readable format
- `z.treeifyError()` - Tree structure format
</methods>
</rule>

<rule>
<name>Functions</name>
<requirement>Define function schemas with input/output types</requirement>
<example type="good">
```typescript
z.function({
	input: [z.string()],
	output: z.number(),
})
```
</example>
</rule>

<rule>
<name>Records</name>
<syntax>`z.record(keyType, valueType)`</syntax>
</rule>

<rule>
<name>ISO Formats</name>
<formats>
- `z.iso.datetime()` - ISO 8601 datetime
- `z.iso.date()` - ISO 8601 date
</formats>
</rule>

<rule>
<name>Additional Features</name>
<features>
- Default: `.default()` applies to output; use `.prefault()` for v3 behavior
- File validation: `z.file().min(1024).max(5*1024*1024).mime(['image/jpeg'])`
- Pipe: `z.pipe(z.string(), z.number())` for transformations
- Async: Use `.check(async (val) => {...})` for async validation
- Arrays: `z.array(z.email())` or `z.email().array()`
- Optional: `.optional()`, `.nullable()`, `.nullish()`
</features>
</rule>

</core-rules>

<new-v4-features>

<feature>
<name>Using @zod/mini for Frontend</name>
<when-to-use>Use @zod/mini in frontend applications where bundle size matters</when-to-use>
<example>
```typescript
// Instead of: import { z } from 'zod/v4'
import { z } from '@zod/mini'

// Same API, 1.9KB gzipped!
export type LoginForm = z.infer<typeof LoginForm>
export const LoginForm = z.object({
  email: z.email(),
  password: z.string().min(8),
})
```
</example>
</feature>

<feature>
<name>.overwrite() Method</name>
<when-to-use>Use when transforming data without changing the inferred type</when-to-use>
<example>
```typescript
// Normalize email without changing type
export type Email = z.infer<typeof Email>
export const Email = z.email().overwrite((email) => email.toLowerCase())

// Before: email could be "USER@EXAMPLE.COM"
// After: email is "user@example.com" but type is still string
```
</example>
</feature>

<feature>
<name>Global Registry</name>
<when-to-use>Use in large apps or plugin systems where schemas need to be shared</when-to-use>
<example>
```typescript
import { z } from 'zod/v4'

// Register a schema globally
z.registry.set('User', z.object({
  id: z.string(),
  email: z.email(),
  name: z.string(),
}))

// Retrieve it anywhere in your app
const UserSchema = z.registry.get('User')

// Use in other schemas
export type Post = z.infer<typeof Post>
export const Post = z.object({
  id: z.string(),
  author: z.registry.get('User'),
  content: z.string(),
})
```
</example>
</feature>

<feature>
<name>Custom Metadata for Form Generation</name>
<when-to-use>Use when building form libraries or schema-driven UI systems</when-to-use>
<example>
```typescript
// Define schema with UI metadata
export type UserProfile = z.infer<typeof UserProfile>
export const UserProfile = z.object({
  name: z.string().meta({
    label: 'Full Name',
    placeholder: 'Enter your full name',
    order: 1,
  }),
  email: z.email().meta({
    label: 'Email Address',
    placeholder: 'you@example.com',
    order: 2,
  }),
  age: z.number().min(18).meta({
    label: 'Age',
    placeholder: '18',
    order: 3,
    helpText: 'Must be 18 or older',
  }),
})

// Use metadata to generate forms
function generateForm(schema: z.ZodObject<any>) {
  const fields = Object.entries(schema.shape).map(([key, field]) => ({
    name: key,
    ...field._def.meta, // Access metadata
  }))
  return fields.sort((a, b) => a.order - b.order)
}
```
</example>
</feature>

<feature>
<name>Pretty Error Messages</name>
<when-to-use>Use for user-friendly error display in production</when-to-use>
<example>
```typescript
import { z } from 'zod/v4'

export type Order = z.infer<typeof Order>
export const Order = z.object({
  items: z.array(z.object({
    productId: z.string(),
    quantity: z.number().min(1),
  })).min(1),
  email: z.email(),
})

const result = Order.safeParse({ items: [], email: 'invalid' })

if (!result.success) {
  // Old way: Complex ZodError object
  console.log(result.error)

  // New way: Pretty printed errors
  console.log(z.prettifyError(result.error))
  /*
  Validation failed:
    - items: Array must contain at least 1 element(s)
    - email: Invalid email
  */

  // Tree format for nested errors
  console.log(z.treeifyError(result.error))
}
```
</example>
</feature>

<feature>
<name>True Recursive Types</name>
<when-to-use>Use for nested data structures like comments, org charts, etc.</when-to-use>
<example>
```typescript
// v4: No more z.lazy() needed!
export type Comment = z.infer<typeof Comment>
export const Comment = z.object({
  id: z.string(),
  text: z.string(),
  author: z.string(),
  replies: z.array(Comment), // Direct recursion!
})

// Works seamlessly
const comment: Comment = {
  id: '1',
  text: 'Great post!',
  author: 'Alice',
  replies: [
    {
      id: '2',
      text: 'Thanks!',
      author: 'Bob',
      replies: [],
    },
  ],
}
```
</example>
</feature>

<feature>
<name>Template Literals</name>
<when-to-use>Use for validating specific string patterns</when-to-use>
<example>
```typescript
// Validate template literal types
export type ColorHex = z.infer<typeof ColorHex>
export const ColorHex = z.template`#${z.string().length(6)}`

ColorHex.parse('#ff0000') // ✓
ColorHex.parse('#f00') // ✗ Too short
ColorHex.parse('ff0000') // ✗ Missing #

// Complex patterns
export type SemVer = z.infer<typeof SemVer>
export const SemVer = z.template`${z.number()}.${z.number()}.${z.number()}`

SemVer.parse('1.2.3') // ✓
```
</example>
</feature>

</new-v4-features>

<quick-reference>

| v3                      | v4                          |
| ----------------------- | --------------------------- |
| z.string().email()      | z.email()                   |
| {message: "err"}        | {error: "err"}              |
| .strict()               | z.strictObject()            |
| .format()               | z.treeifyError()            |
| z.string().datetime()   | z.iso.datetime()            |
| .args().returns()       | {input:[...], output:...}   |
| .superRefine()          | .check()                    |
| ctx.addIssue()          | ctx.issues.push()           |
| z.ZodIssueCode.custom   | 'custom'                    |
</quick-reference>

<complete-example>
```typescript
import { z } from 'zod/v4'

/** User registration */
export type UserReg = z.infer<typeof UserReg>
export const UserReg = z.object({
	email: z.email(),
	password: z
		.string()
		.min(8)
		.check((pwd) => /[A-Z]/.test(pwd) && /\d/.test(pwd), { error: 'Need uppercase & number' }),
	age: z.number().min(18),
})

/** Function with input/output types */
export type ProcessUser = z.infer<typeof ProcessUser>
export const ProcessUser = z.function({
	input: [UserReg],
	output: z.object({
		id: z.string(),
		createdAt: z.iso.datetime(),
	}),
})
```
</complete-example>

</zod-v4-guidelines>
